`proxy` / `proxyRequest` always stripped `Content-Length` from upstream responses. Byte-range responses (e.g. MP4 `206` through Nitro) lost their length even though the body was relayed unchanged. Related to #891.

This PR forwards the upstream `Content-Length` only when it still describes the body the client receives. It stays dropped when:

- **The body may have been decoded.** `Content-Encoding` is anything other than `identity`. Node, Bun and Deno decompress in `fetch` but keep the compressed length.
- **The response is CORS-filtered** (`response.type === "cors"`). Browsers expose `Content-Length` but can hide `Content-Encoding`.
- **`Transfer-Encoding` is present.** Internal (`/path`) targets skip the HTTP parser that rejects this combination. Forwarding the length there could hang or silently truncate the response.
- **The status is `204`.** RFC 9110 forbids a length on 204.
- **There is no body,** unless the downstream request is `HEAD` or the status is `304`. For those, the representation length is meaningful. This stops a GET proxied upstream as HEAD from promising bytes it never sends.

`content-length` moves out of the static `ignoredResponseHeaders` set. It is still stripped when the upstream `Connection` header nominates it.

> [!NOTE]
> Proxied responses can now carry `Content-Length`. Code that rewrites a proxied body (a proxy `onResponse` hook or middleware that copies the headers) must drop `content-length` itself.

<details>
<summary>Minimal reproduction</summary>

Save as `repro.mjs`, then run `npm install h3@2.0.8 && node repro.mjs` with Node.js 24. To check a local build, run `pnpm build`, then `node repro.mjs file:///path/to/h3/dist/_entries/node.mjs`.

```js
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { createServer } from "node:http";

const { H3, proxyRequest, toNodeHandler } = await import(process.argv[2] || "h3");
const app = new H3();
const server = createServer(toNodeHandler(app));
await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
const origin = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`;

app.get("/upstream", () => new Response(new Uint8Array([0, 255]), {
  status: 206,
  headers: {
    "content-type": "video/mp4",
    "content-length": "2",
    "content-range": "bytes 0-1/100",
    "accept-ranges": "bytes",
  },
}));
app.get("/proxy", (event) => proxyRequest(event, `${origin}/upstream`));

try {
  const results = [];
  for (const path of ["/upstream", "/proxy"]) {
    const response = await fetch(`${origin}${path}`, { headers: { range: "bytes=0-1" } });
    results.push({
      path,
      status: response.status,
      contentLength: response.headers.get("content-length"),
      bytes: Array.from(new Uint8Array(await response.arrayBuffer())),
    });
  }
  console.table(results);
  assert.equal(results[1].contentLength, results[0].contentLength);
  assert.deepEqual(results[1].bytes, results[0].bytes);
} finally {
  await new Promise((resolve) => server.close(resolve));
}
```

On 2.0.8 the proxied response has no `Content-Length`, so the assertion fails.

</details>

## Tests

`test/proxy-response.test.ts` runs in both web and node modes. It covers:

- 200/206 ranges and `identity` encoding
- HEAD and 304 lengths
- a HEAD fetch serving a GET
- 204 for GET and HEAD
- `Transfer-Encoding` conflicts
- gzip, deflate and br decompression
- `Connection`-nominated stripping
- CORS-filtered responses

`pnpm test` passes (3,136 tests).

